    <fileDesc>
      <titleStmt>
        <title type="main" xml:lang="san-Latn">Halāsyamāhātmya</title>
        <title type="main" xml:lang="san-Deva">हालास्यमाहात्म्यम्</title>
        <title type="sub" xml:lang="eng">Chapter-level correspondence skeleton: 44 adhyayas
        aligned to the Tamil Tiruvilaiyadal Puranam, demonstration edition</title>
        <editor role="encoder">
          <persName>
            <forename>Ani</forename>
            <surname>Chandrashekhar</surname>
          </persName>
        </editor>
        <respStmt>
          <resp>TEI encoding, chapter-correspondence research and pipeline</resp>
          <name>Ani Chandrashekhar</name>
        </respStmt>
      </titleStmt>
      <editionStmt>
        <edition n="0.1">Demonstration edition: chapter-level structure and
        cross-language alignment only, replacing the project's former Kanchipuram
        sample corpus.</edition>
      </editionStmt>
      <publicationStmt>
        <publisher>Unpublished demonstration repository</publisher>
        <availability status="free">
          <licence target="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">
            <p>Encoding and schema released under CC BY 4.0.</p>
          </licence>
        </availability>
        <date when="2026-09-20"/>
      </publicationStmt>
      <notesStmt>
        <note type="provenance-warning" xml:lang="eng">
          <p>IMPORTANT. This file replaces the project's former Kanchipuram sample
          corpus with a demonstration corpus for the Halasya Mahatmya / Tiruvilaiyadal
          Puranam pair. Two things are real here and one is not:</p>
          <p><emph>Real:</emph> the adhyaya numbering and each chapter's Sanskrit title
          (<mentioned>head</mentioned>) were read from the OCR'd विषयानुक्रमणिका
          (table of contents) of the S.V. Radhakrishna Sastri edition digitised on
          archive.org — see <ptr target="#HM-archive-bibl"/>. The OCR recovered legible
          titles for 62 of the work's 71 adhyayas; the 44 encoded here are the subset the
          research pass could confidently match to a Tamil padalam. The
          <mentioned>@corresp</mentioned> links to
          <ref target="tiruvilaiyadal-puranam.xml">tiruvilaiyadal-puranam.xml</ref> record
          that matching.</p>
          <p><emph>Not real:</emph> the exact archive.org item identifier/URL was not
          retained during the research pass and must be re-found and attached to
          <ptr target="#HM-archive-bibl"/> before this citation is treated as complete.
          The verse text inside each <mentioned>lg</mentioned> is
          <emph>synthetic placeholder material</emph>, a short generic invocatory couplet
          reused across chapters to exercise the encoding model — it is not a
          transcription of any adhyaya's actual content and must not be cited as one. See
          <ref target="../../docs/encoding-guidelines.md">encoding guidelines</ref>,
          section "Replacing the sample corpus".</p>
        </note>
        <note type="alignment" xml:lang="eng">
          <p>Adhyayas 1-4 (invocation/kshetra-glorification frame material) and the
          अनुबन्धः appendix after adhyaya 71 (unrelated hymns) are deliberately excluded
          from this corpus as non-narrative. Adhyayas 70-71 fall after the point where the
          Tamil 64-story cycle ends and have no confirmed Tamil counterpart, so are also
          excluded. Adhyayas with OCR-illegible titles (3, 4, 11, 19, 20, 23, 26, 34, 37,
          59, 61) are absent rather than guessed at; a second pass against archive.org's
          page-image viewer, rather than its plain-text OCR layer, would be needed to
          recover them.</p>
        </note>
      </notesStmt>
